00:00:06.314 --> 00:00:08.730
MICHAEL BASILYAN: So the talk
is called Research Workloads

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:09.600
on Preemptible VMs.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.620
I'm Michael Basilyan,
product manager at Google.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:14.729
I'm joined by two friends from
Two Sigma, Chris and Rodrigo.

00:00:14.729 --> 00:00:16.770
They'll give a little bit
more of an introduction

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.100
in the second half when they
talk about their workloads

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:21.690
on preemptible VMs.

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:22.690
So here's the outline.

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:23.820
I'm going to talk
for a few minutes

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:25.560
about what preemptible VMs
are, and how they work,

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:27.020
and what the general concept is.

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:30.724
And then the Two Sigma
guys will tell their story.

00:00:30.724 --> 00:00:31.591
Cool.

00:00:31.591 --> 00:00:32.090
All right.

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:34.961
So if I had to tell you what a
preemptible VM is in one slide,

00:00:34.961 --> 00:00:36.710
and if there's one
thing that you remember

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:39.610
from my half of the talk,
is that a preemptible VM

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:41.900
is a regular VM, like
any other VM that you

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:43.620
can get on Compute Engine.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:44.750
There's just two caveats.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:48.020
One is that it cannot run
for more than 24 hours.

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:52.031
We limit the lifetime and the
uptime of the VM for 24 hours.

00:00:52.031 --> 00:00:54.530
And the other is that we reserve
the right to take them away

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:56.520
with 30 seconds of
notice at any point.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:57.728
It's right there in the name.

00:00:57.728 --> 00:00:59.750
That's what makes
it preemptible.

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:03.050
The upside of it is that they
only cost $0.01 per core hour.

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:05.720
And that includes
Intel Skylakes.

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:12.650
That's up to 80% cheaper
than a regular GCE VM.

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:16.220
If you attach a GPU to
that preemptible VM,

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:20.690
it'll be about 70% cheaper
than a regular GPU.

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:23.570
If you attach a local SSD
to that preemptible VM,

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:27.410
it'll be around 40% cheaper
than a local SSD attached

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:28.660
to a standard VM.

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:30.620
And local SSD, you
might have heard already

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:33.635
at some point today or in
one of the earlier talks,

00:01:33.635 --> 00:01:38.850
are high performance attached
storage for virtual machines.

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:40.850
The other highlight is
that prices are constant.

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:42.230
There's no bidding of any kind.

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:43.040
This is the price.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:44.720
What you see is what you
get in terms of price.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:45.553
That doesn't change.

00:01:49.601 --> 00:01:50.100
Cool.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.624
So what can you do
with compute that's

00:01:52.624 --> 00:01:54.040
just sort of
randomly disappearing

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:55.552
with 30 seconds of notice?

00:01:55.552 --> 00:01:57.010
There's actually
quite a few things

00:01:57.010 --> 00:01:59.380
that we've seen people do.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:01.570
Really anything that's
embarrassingly parallel,

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:03.850
or pleasantly parallel
as some people say,

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:05.854
anything that is high
throughput, or is composed

00:02:05.854 --> 00:02:07.270
of a lot of
independent tasks that

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:09.729
can be solved independently
where losing a single machine

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:13.030
doesn't mean that
everything else disappears,

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:15.340
or if you can figure
out how to capture state

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:16.990
for your computations.

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:18.670
So Monte Carlo
simulations, where

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:22.810
different machines are solving
different input conditions.

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:25.810
Image understanding-- rendering,
immediate transcoding, anything

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:26.870
really to do with images.

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:29.380
If you imagine a use case
where you have 1,000 images,

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:30.880
and you want to
assign each of those

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:33.640
to one of 100
preemptible VMs to do

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:36.366
some processing on that
image, if that VM goes away

00:02:36.366 --> 00:02:38.740
because it gets preempted,
you don't really lose anything

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:39.700
except for that image.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:41.710
And you can reschedule
that work if your scheduler

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:42.751
knows how to handle that.

00:02:42.751 --> 00:02:44.680
And I'll talk about
that in a little bit.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:47.290
Data analysis, of course--

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:51.070
our Dataproc product
supports preemptible VMs.

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:54.825
Genomics and health
workloads-- if you

00:02:54.825 --> 00:02:56.950
search the web for Broad
Institute preemptible VMs,

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:59.614
you'll see a write
up of their use case.

00:02:59.614 --> 00:03:01.780
Financial analysis, of
course-- and you'll hear more

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:03.310
about that in the second half.

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:05.286
And many, many others,
including physics, math,

00:03:05.286 --> 00:03:06.160
chemistry, and so on.

00:03:08.675 --> 00:03:10.300
And to be clear,
people are using these

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:12.260
to solve very real workloads.

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:15.400
So on any given day we have
10,000 to 50,000 core clusters

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:18.930
of preemptible VMs being
used, multiple ones.

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:22.420
And the largest we've ever
seen is an MIT math professor

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:26.410
doing some research in a
weekend on 580,000 cores.

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:29.140
So this was around
200 to 300 core years

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:30.400
of compute in a weekend.

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:34.700
OK.

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:37.360
Using them is relatively
straightforward and easy.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:39.110
There's just one flag
that you have to do.

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:43.220
Like I said, they are
like regular GCVMs.

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:45.140
You configure them
like a regular GCVM.

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:47.180
All you do is add a
dash, dash preemptible

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:48.370
to your command line.

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:50.970
Or in the UI, you just
select preemptibility on.

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:53.210
Or in the API, you select
preemptibility to be true.

00:03:56.339 --> 00:03:57.755
Generally, of
course, you're going

00:03:57.755 --> 00:03:59.629
to be creating more than
one of these things.

00:03:59.629 --> 00:04:02.380
And so that's where things get
a little bit more interesting.

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:05.090
But fortunately, there's a lot
of tools that make it easy.

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:08.680
We have our GCE managed
instance groups that

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:11.350
can help you manage
instances of preemptible VMs,

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:14.500
and recreate them once
they get preempted.

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:16.329
We have a published
solution on our website

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:19.019
for how to use HTCondor,
which is a very popular job

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:21.720
scheduling tool with
preemptible VMs.

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:25.437
Kubernetes and GKE, of course,
support preemptible VMs.

00:04:25.437 --> 00:04:27.520
And in the second half,
you'll hear more about Two

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:30.700
Sigma's Cook job scheduler that
supports preemptible VMs as

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:33.410
well.

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:35.980
So I wanted to give you guys
some best practices for how

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:37.980
to use preemptible VMs
to make the most of them,

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:40.660
and to minimize the preemptions.

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:42.910
So like old school
telephone companies,

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:44.146
nights and weekends are best.

00:04:44.146 --> 00:04:45.520
Obviously you want
to avoid times

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:48.995
when the data centers are busy.

00:04:48.995 --> 00:04:51.370
You want to architect your
system for regular preemption.

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:53.328
You should expect that
preemptions will happen.

00:04:53.328 --> 00:04:55.420
They're not a one-off
rare situation.

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:59.350
We see on historical rates,
5% to 15% preemption rates.

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:04.240
You should know that this will
change as a function of the VMs

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:06.670
that you build, the zones
you use, and time of day.

00:05:10.799 --> 00:05:12.340
And of course, you
should be prepared

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:14.590
to move regions and zones
if preemption rates get

00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:16.045
really high in a place.

00:05:16.045 --> 00:05:19.780
But that's good cloud
practice anyway.

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:22.990
Beyond that, use shutdown
scripts and checkpoints

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:25.622
to save state if that's
appropriate for your workload.

00:05:25.622 --> 00:05:27.580
I'm talking about standard
GCE shutdown scripts

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:29.350
that are just part of Linux.

00:05:29.350 --> 00:05:31.180
The smaller and
less choosy the VM,

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:33.500
the less likely it
is to be preempted.

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:38.440
So a 64 core preemptible
VM with local SSD and GPU

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:40.660
is much more likely to
be in somebody's way

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:45.550
than a 16 core preemptible VM.

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:48.520
On the website we also have
a bunch of best practices

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:50.610
that outline a lot of this
with a lot more detail

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:52.670
than you can read.

00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:53.534
Cool.

00:05:53.534 --> 00:05:55.700
And I'm going to turn it
over to the Two Sigma team.

