00:07:03.484 --> 00:07:05.400
And then some of the
challenges and takeaways,

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:09.820
which is kind of the interesting
things for most people.

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:12.470
So batch research process.

00:07:12.470 --> 00:07:13.820
Two Sigma's a quant hedge fund.

00:07:13.820 --> 00:07:15.236
And so how does a
quant hedge fund

00:07:15.236 --> 00:07:17.430
make money and
survive in the world?

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:20.430
You may envision as a model or
that comes up with this idea,

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:20.969
oh, eureka.

00:07:20.969 --> 00:07:22.510
Here's how we're
going to make money.

00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:25.380
Draws a technique on a
whiteboard, and, poof,

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:26.930
it becomes production.

00:07:26.930 --> 00:07:29.200
Because that's how it
works in the movies, right?

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:31.830
So not exactly.

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:33.360
The modeling workflow
actually looks

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:36.774
fairly similar to a
typical developer workflow.

00:07:36.774 --> 00:07:38.440
There's an idea of
what they want to do.

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:39.523
They do some work with it.

00:07:39.523 --> 00:07:42.450
They do some data analysis
and shifting of data

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:43.880
to figure out what makes sense.

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:45.630
And they try to write
some techniques out.

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:48.374
And then they want to simulate
it, usually research back

00:07:48.374 --> 00:07:49.290
testing in the market.

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:51.990
Like, hey, how would this
have performed over the past n

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:53.295
number of years?

00:07:53.295 --> 00:07:55.420
And then from there, once
they actually figure out,

00:07:55.420 --> 00:07:57.211
hey, this is going to
actually work for us,

00:07:57.211 --> 00:07:59.240
they do a typical
review process.

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:01.350
Similar to a code review,
they write papers up.

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:02.879
They present to their peers.

00:08:02.879 --> 00:08:03.670
They figure it out.

00:08:03.670 --> 00:08:06.060
And it becomes a production
product at some point.

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:07.650
A lot of what we
use Google Cloud for

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:10.590
is that technique in data
in the simulation area.

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:12.070
A lot of the data
analysis tools,

00:08:12.070 --> 00:08:13.860
a lot of the simulation
back testing,

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:16.350
we're running on Google today.

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:18.810
And especially the
simulation of preemptible VMs

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:22.497
is where GCE really
shines for us.

00:08:22.497 --> 00:08:24.080
So a bit about our
distributed compute

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:26.150
environment, and kind
of over the history

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:28.310
over the past 15 or 16 years.

00:08:28.310 --> 00:08:30.620
As legend has it, when
Two Sigma was founded,

00:08:30.620 --> 00:08:33.710
a couple of founders, a couple
of early employees time share

00:08:33.710 --> 00:08:35.710
compute based on a whiteboard.

00:08:35.710 --> 00:08:39.049
Literally just on a white board,
hey, Bob has it this week.

00:08:39.049 --> 00:08:41.159
Susie has it next
week, et cetera.

00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:43.700
And I guess that worked for a
few years, at least three years

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:46.010
or so, from what I'm told.

00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:49.470
Early 2004 the team decided to
work on an internal scheduling

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:49.970
system.

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:52.745
Really just we want
to be technologists.

00:08:52.745 --> 00:08:55.120
We want to figure out a better
system than a white board,

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:56.234
quite frankly.

00:08:56.234 --> 00:08:57.650
So that worked
well for apparently

00:08:57.650 --> 00:08:59.360
eight years or so,
the internal one.

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:01.940
And around 2012, Mesos
came on the scene

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:03.740
and really captured
our attention.

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:05.990
It solved a lot of the really
interesting lower level

00:09:05.990 --> 00:09:06.490
problems.

00:09:06.490 --> 00:09:08.540
How do you do
resource management?

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:09.804
How do you do scheduling?

00:09:09.804 --> 00:09:11.720
Those are the types of
things, like how do you

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:13.553
execute a job on this
host, aren't something

00:09:13.553 --> 00:09:16.370
that are a core competency of
what Two Sigma wanted to be

00:09:16.370 --> 00:09:17.870
or wants to be.

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:20.584
So we really adopted Mesos
full speed with that.

00:09:20.584 --> 00:09:23.000
But we still want to maintain
its fair share of scheduling

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:23.270
things.

00:09:23.270 --> 00:09:25.040
So even back in the early days
of a whiteboard, we're like,

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:26.930
hey, we want
efficient utilization.

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:28.910
We want it shared
across everyone.

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:30.464
So we decided to
write what is now

00:09:30.464 --> 00:09:31.880
known as the Cook
framework, which

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:35.350
is a framework for fair share
scheduling of jobs on Mesos.

00:09:35.350 --> 00:09:37.670
And we've been using that
ever since 2012 when we first

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:40.010
started writing it.

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:43.860
So 2016, cloud
computing two years ago

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:45.530
was definitely a
mainstream concept.

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:47.821
We decided we really wanted
to start working with that,

00:09:47.821 --> 00:09:50.450
and figuring out, how do
we use elastic capacity?

00:09:50.450 --> 00:09:53.540
Up to that point, we'd always
had physical data centers.

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:54.980
Everything is,
here's the hardware

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:56.570
and here's what we can use.

00:09:56.570 --> 00:09:58.130
So we started making
a lot of changes

00:09:58.130 --> 00:10:00.170
to connect our data
centers to the cloud,

00:10:00.170 --> 00:10:01.790
and then changes
to Cook and Mesos

00:10:01.790 --> 00:10:04.910
to work in these environments.

00:10:04.910 --> 00:10:09.206
And then now, mid-2018, we have
real dynamic cloud compute,

00:10:09.206 --> 00:10:11.080
which has really been
very beneficial for us,

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:13.190
to be able to say,
what is our workload?

00:10:13.190 --> 00:10:14.300
What can we do?

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:16.520
We're not limiting
our modelers based

00:10:16.520 --> 00:10:18.787
on the compute that we
can get to them anymore.

00:10:18.787 --> 00:10:20.620
But there's a caveat
though that we're going

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:22.040
to talk about in a minute.

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:24.500
Even though we have a
classic dynamic compute,

00:10:24.500 --> 00:10:27.060
we don't have dynamic storage,
or networking, or other things

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:27.920
still.

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:30.440
We'll talk about in a few
minutes about how we're still

00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:35.540
running hybrid cloud with
Google and other data centers.

00:10:35.540 --> 00:10:36.470
There we go.

00:10:36.470 --> 00:10:40.250
So a little bit of capacity
over time, which kind of just

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:42.890
symbolizes what I just
mentioned, where over time is

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:44.355
a very step function.

00:10:44.355 --> 00:10:46.730
On this date we decided to
order a whole bunch of servers

00:10:46.730 --> 00:10:47.790
and put in a data center.

00:10:47.790 --> 00:10:50.570
And that's how it lasted
for 15 years or so.

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:54.260
And mid-2016, you start to
see a lot of dynamic spikes

00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:55.772
based on user workload.

00:10:55.772 --> 00:10:57.230
This is really when
cloud computing

00:10:57.230 --> 00:10:58.200
became a thing for us.

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:02.360
So one, it opened up compute
ability for our modelers,

00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:04.700
but also proved a theory
that we had years before,

00:11:04.700 --> 00:11:06.630
which is that our
demand isn't linear.

00:11:06.630 --> 00:11:09.320
It's not, oh, here's what
we're going to use today.

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:11.300
It's a lot more
opportunistic based

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:15.200
on what phase of
research somebody is in.

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:17.689
And then there's that.

00:11:17.689 --> 00:11:18.980
So that's a bit of the history.

00:11:18.980 --> 00:11:20.920
That's about how
we got to today.

00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:22.080
But what are our users?

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:23.904
So Rodrigo and I,
we work on what's

00:11:23.904 --> 00:11:25.070
called platform engineering.

00:11:25.070 --> 00:11:28.127
So we deal with a lot
of the lower level,

00:11:28.127 --> 00:11:29.210
how do you build a system?

00:11:29.210 --> 00:11:31.430
How do you get resources
available to somebody?

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:32.638
And there are teams above us.

00:11:32.638 --> 00:11:34.290
So we think of
modeling engineering,

00:11:34.290 --> 00:11:36.920
which the next step above
us, they use our platform

00:11:36.920 --> 00:11:38.254
as a building block.

00:11:38.254 --> 00:11:39.920
Modeling engineering
does a lot of stuff

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:42.110
to work with techniques,
with data analysis,

00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:45.440
a lot of like how
to run a simulation.

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:47.540
Then above them we have
quantitative engineers.

00:11:47.540 --> 00:11:49.820
Quantitative engineers,
they work a lot closer

00:11:49.820 --> 00:11:51.020
to the modelers.

00:11:51.020 --> 00:11:52.985
And they work with actually
writing the models,

00:11:52.985 --> 00:11:54.860
actually writing
techniques, writing tactics,

00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:57.692
writing the actual code that
will eventually do trading.

00:11:57.692 --> 00:11:59.150
And then finally,
we have modelers,

00:11:59.150 --> 00:12:01.108
who are our researchers
that are going through,

00:12:01.108 --> 00:12:04.130
trying to find information
that is tradable

00:12:04.130 --> 00:12:06.880
and actually usable.

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:09.220
So we have a bit
of a description

00:12:09.220 --> 00:12:10.370
of kind of the workloads.

00:12:10.370 --> 00:12:12.370
And this is where fair
share scheduling actually

00:12:12.370 --> 00:12:14.119
comes back to us and
is kind of important,

00:12:14.119 --> 00:12:17.011
is that even though we have
all the dynamic compute,

00:12:17.011 --> 00:12:18.760
I mentioned we don't
have dynamic storage.

00:12:18.760 --> 00:12:20.900
We don't have dynamic
networking yet.

00:12:20.900 --> 00:12:23.320
So we still want to
be able to fair share

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:24.580
our resources with people.

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:26.170
And we also still care
about things like quotas.

00:12:26.170 --> 00:12:27.760
We still care what the bill
looks like every month.

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:30.131
We care about a lot of
those types of things.

00:12:30.131 --> 00:12:32.380
So when you think of the
workloads that we're running,

00:12:32.380 --> 00:12:34.338
again, modeling engineers
who are building just

00:12:34.338 --> 00:12:37.500
right above us, they're doing
scheduling, Cook schedulers.

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:38.710
They're doing other things.

00:12:38.710 --> 00:12:41.620
They need a little bit of
capacity, a few hundred

00:12:41.620 --> 00:12:47.500
gigabytes, 10s, 20s of vCPUs
to test out their work.

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:50.092
The quantitative engineers,
though, because they're

00:12:50.092 --> 00:12:52.300
working closer to models,
they actually want to test,

00:12:52.300 --> 00:12:54.907
hey, does this
model actually work?

00:12:54.907 --> 00:12:56.740
From a technical
standpoint, they need more.

00:12:56.740 --> 00:12:58.272
They need to do more testing.

00:12:58.272 --> 00:12:59.980
And then our modelers,
there are probably

00:12:59.980 --> 00:13:01.879
an infinite number of
data sources and things

00:13:01.879 --> 00:13:02.920
to trade on in the world.

00:13:02.920 --> 00:13:04.794
And our modelers want
to run every single one

00:13:04.794 --> 00:13:07.180
of those, which, of
course, isn't possible.

00:13:07.180 --> 00:13:11.460
So they are the highest demand
of usage across our company.

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:13.870
Which actually
these slides all are

00:13:13.870 --> 00:13:16.630
showing that, kind of what we
see from our internal usage

00:13:16.630 --> 00:13:18.972
patterns.

00:13:18.972 --> 00:13:20.930
And on that note, I'll
throw it over to Rodrigo

00:13:20.930 --> 00:13:23.495
to talk about how we actually
build platforms in GCP.

00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:29.460
RODRIGO MANYARI:
Thank you, Chris.

00:13:29.460 --> 00:13:33.660
So Chris just talked about
kind of how the environment has

00:13:33.660 --> 00:13:36.210
evolved over time,
and also capacity,

00:13:36.210 --> 00:13:38.700
how it used to be this
very nice step function.

00:13:38.700 --> 00:13:40.950
And in recent years
it's more like bursting.

00:13:40.950 --> 00:13:43.860
And also how we kind
of view our users,

00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:46.470
how they share this platform.

00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:48.750
And we can think about all
of this as very cool data.

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:50.833
And we can do some more
analysis, and dig into it.

00:13:50.833 --> 00:13:52.560
Or we can take that
as requirements,

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:56.330
which is what I think we've been
doing for the last two years.

00:13:56.330 --> 00:13:58.677
So when you think
about this environment,

00:13:58.677 --> 00:14:00.010
we really have two requirements.

00:14:00.010 --> 00:14:02.890
One, following the assumption
that demand is very bursty,

00:14:02.890 --> 00:14:06.330
we need to be able to scale
up and down automatically.

00:14:06.330 --> 00:14:12.786
And two, everyone is
sharing this platform,

00:14:12.786 --> 00:14:14.160
but everyone has
different needs.

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:17.340
So we're not in a position
where we kind of impose

00:14:17.340 --> 00:14:19.020
people what resources they get.

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:22.140
We want this cluster
to be driven by demand.

00:14:22.140 --> 00:14:26.970
And as we started to move
these workloads to the cloud,

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:31.410
we kind of came up with
two guiding principles.

00:14:31.410 --> 00:14:34.290
The first thing that I want
to talk about is security.

00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:38.490
We take security very
seriously at Two Sigma.

00:14:38.490 --> 00:14:39.990
As you can imagine,
this environment

00:14:39.990 --> 00:14:41.406
is one of the most
sensitive ones,

00:14:41.406 --> 00:14:44.220
because it has some good
stuff going in there.

00:14:44.220 --> 00:14:47.624
So we stick to GCE and some
core networking products,

00:14:47.624 --> 00:14:50.040
like interconnects, to provide
connectivity back and forth

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:52.200
between Two Sigma and GCP.

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:54.960
I'm not going to talk more about
security in this presentation,

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:58.830
because other guys, Nick
and Michael from Two Sigma,

00:14:58.830 --> 00:15:03.000
are giving enough
talks on the topic.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:04.980
The next thing that
I want to talk about,

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:08.100
though, is kind
of the other thing

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:09.720
that we always try
very hard to keep

00:15:09.720 --> 00:15:14.260
in mind, which is elasticity,
add value to the users,

00:15:14.260 --> 00:15:17.970
add value to the business, and
do so in a cost effective way.

00:15:17.970 --> 00:15:20.040
Because we've been
operating data centers

00:15:20.040 --> 00:15:22.260
for 10 to 15 years,
we know how much

00:15:22.260 --> 00:15:24.840
it costs to run
these environments.

00:15:24.840 --> 00:15:27.960
And we cannot just go to the
cloud and start spending money

00:15:27.960 --> 00:15:30.000
like crazy because it's
this shiny new thing,

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:31.680
or because everyone is doing it.

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:34.050
We have to be able
to add real value.

00:15:34.050 --> 00:15:37.020
And we need to be able to do
that in a cost effective way.

00:15:37.020 --> 00:15:40.050
Now, preemptible VMs
and custom machine types

00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:42.210
is what really
allows us to do that.

00:15:42.210 --> 00:15:44.562
We get the 80% flat discount.

00:15:44.562 --> 00:15:46.020
And then with custom
machine types,

00:15:46.020 --> 00:15:49.142
we create exactly the
resources that Cook needs--

00:15:49.142 --> 00:15:50.600
I'm going to talk
more about Cook--

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:54.330
but that Cook needs to
schedule these jobs.

00:15:54.330 --> 00:15:56.079
Now, this is a fairly
large environment.

00:15:56.079 --> 00:15:58.620
And I don't want to be on the
hook, creating and deleting VMs

00:15:58.620 --> 00:15:59.370
all the time.

00:15:59.370 --> 00:16:00.920
And Chris, I'm sure,
doesn't want me

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:02.500
to be on the hook to do that.

00:16:02.500 --> 00:16:06.900
So we need an autoscaler, which
is a product that we've been

00:16:06.900 --> 00:16:09.480
working on since early 2016.

00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:11.380
So now, if you take
the autoscaler,

00:16:11.380 --> 00:16:14.430
you take preemptible VMs, and
you take custom machine types,

00:16:14.430 --> 00:16:17.430
now you get all
the building blocks

00:16:17.430 --> 00:16:19.980
to build this platform
where you can scale

00:16:19.980 --> 00:16:26.950
to a couple of Two Sigma data
center sizes for, let's say,

00:16:26.950 --> 00:16:28.530
a single user.

00:16:28.530 --> 00:16:31.410
Before 2016, they had to
share all this platform

00:16:31.410 --> 00:16:32.880
between each other.

00:16:32.880 --> 00:16:36.150
Now, it's just a matter of
putting stuff in some queue.

00:16:36.150 --> 00:16:39.390
And then you're going to
get capacity eventually.

00:16:39.390 --> 00:16:41.126
But before talking
about autoscaling,

00:16:41.126 --> 00:16:42.750
I want to talk about
Cook a little bit.

00:16:42.750 --> 00:16:45.240
Because Cook sits at
the top of the stack.

00:16:45.240 --> 00:16:47.250
And without Cook, there
really isn't much.

00:16:47.250 --> 00:16:50.460
What are these VMs good for
if nothing is running on them?

00:16:50.460 --> 00:16:53.790
So Cook is a Mesos framework.

00:16:53.790 --> 00:16:57.000
Early versions were
developed back in 2012.

00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:59.040
It's open source.

00:16:59.040 --> 00:17:03.040
And it has this very core
concept of fair scheduling

00:17:03.040 --> 00:17:08.339
that we use both on-prem
and in the cloud,

00:17:08.339 --> 00:17:10.495
but just for different reasons.

00:17:10.495 --> 00:17:12.660
Fair scheduling
essentially just guarantees

00:17:12.660 --> 00:17:16.020
that no one user takes over
an entire cluster, which

00:17:16.020 --> 00:17:18.486
is super important when
you're running on-prem.

00:17:18.486 --> 00:17:20.069
But let me give you
an example I think

00:17:20.069 --> 00:17:22.200
that's going to be a
lot easier to understand

00:17:22.200 --> 00:17:23.819
than just me talking about it.

00:17:23.819 --> 00:17:27.150
So suppose user red here has
been running overnight a bunch

00:17:27.150 --> 00:17:28.800
of jobs, and he got lucky.

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:30.140
He got the entire cluster.

00:17:30.140 --> 00:17:32.650
But it's 7:00, 8:00
AM New York time.

00:17:32.650 --> 00:17:38.190
And now we have like three
modelers that want to run jobs.

00:17:38.190 --> 00:17:40.320
There is one kind of
obvious problem here.

00:17:40.320 --> 00:17:43.470
There is no capacity to run
all these additional jobs.

00:17:43.470 --> 00:17:44.790
But there is another problem.

00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:47.340
Let's say we make up
some capacity somehow.

00:17:47.340 --> 00:17:51.150
Now we need a way to
determine who goes first.

00:17:51.150 --> 00:17:53.100
And we can do some
kind of round robin.

00:17:53.100 --> 00:17:54.550
We can do first
come, first serve.

00:17:54.550 --> 00:17:55.980
We can do random.

00:17:55.980 --> 00:17:58.890
Or we can do kind of
fair sharing, which

00:17:58.890 --> 00:17:59.940
is what Cook does.

00:17:59.940 --> 00:18:02.460
So essentially it's going
to take this virtual queue.

00:18:02.460 --> 00:18:05.210
So each one of these
three virtual queues,

00:18:05.210 --> 00:18:09.210
it's going to merge them
into one global queue.

00:18:09.210 --> 00:18:11.790
And then it's going to
order them by fairness.

00:18:11.790 --> 00:18:15.390
And fairness here
means it's going

00:18:15.390 --> 00:18:18.234
to look at, what's your current
usage, what's your backlog.

00:18:18.234 --> 00:18:19.650
And then based on
that, it's going

00:18:19.650 --> 00:18:21.680
to determine some
kind of ordering.

00:18:21.680 --> 00:18:25.200
Then in the background, it's
going to preempt some red jobs.

00:18:25.200 --> 00:18:27.490
And then it's going to
schedule everything else.

00:18:27.490 --> 00:18:29.620
There is a downside to
this kind of situation,

00:18:29.620 --> 00:18:32.530
which is we lost
some computation

00:18:32.530 --> 00:18:34.211
time that we've already done.

00:18:34.211 --> 00:18:35.210
But it's not a big deal.

00:18:35.210 --> 00:18:37.450
And I think it's much,
much better than just

00:18:37.450 --> 00:18:40.000
making people wait.

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:44.720
Eventually those jobs are going
to get put back in the queue.

00:18:44.720 --> 00:18:47.189
Now, you might be thinking,
we're at a cloud conference.

00:18:47.189 --> 00:18:49.480
Why don't you just create
more VMs and be done with it?

00:18:49.480 --> 00:18:51.140
You don't need to
preempt anyone.

00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:53.500
Which is kind of fair.

00:18:53.500 --> 00:18:56.237
And it kind of works in theory.

00:18:56.237 --> 00:18:57.820
We are less constrained
with capacity.

00:18:57.820 --> 00:19:00.040
We have access to
more machine types.

00:19:00.040 --> 00:19:01.780
But now by having
this elastic cluster,

00:19:01.780 --> 00:19:04.000
we created the
problem for ourselves.

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:07.040
Now we have this
ramp up latency.

00:19:07.040 --> 00:19:11.380
So suppose there's like, I don't
know, 10,000 jobs in the queue.

00:19:11.380 --> 00:19:15.130
And somehow we magically
decided we need 1,000 VMs.

00:19:15.130 --> 00:19:20.170
If I send 1,000 requests
to GCP, well, first of all,

00:19:20.170 --> 00:19:22.194
there was API rate limitings.

00:19:22.194 --> 00:19:24.610
So I cannot just fire 1,000
requests all at the same time.

00:19:24.610 --> 00:19:26.485
And then there is no
guarantee that I'm going

00:19:26.485 --> 00:19:27.940
to get the 1,000 VMs right now.

00:19:27.940 --> 00:19:30.820
And even if I could get
them right now, remember,

00:19:30.820 --> 00:19:33.300
we work in a very
secure environment.

00:19:33.300 --> 00:19:35.319
These VMs need to
get an identity.

00:19:35.319 --> 00:19:37.360
They need to register to
other security services.

00:19:37.360 --> 00:19:38.651
They need to register to Mesos.

00:19:38.651 --> 00:19:41.290
And then Cook eventually
needs to schedule these jobs.

00:19:41.290 --> 00:19:45.310
So all of these steps, they take
time, a couple minutes here,

00:19:45.310 --> 00:19:46.350
a couple minutes there.

00:19:46.350 --> 00:19:49.700
And then when you
add up everything,

00:19:49.700 --> 00:19:51.400
it's time that is
non-negligible.

00:19:51.400 --> 00:19:53.609
And the user can
actually feel it.

00:19:53.609 --> 00:19:54.650
So we want to avoid that.

00:19:54.650 --> 00:19:59.740
And Cook and fair sharing
is kind of the thing

00:19:59.740 --> 00:20:01.330
that we use to
solve this problem.

00:20:01.330 --> 00:20:03.550
It allows us to share
resources as they come up,

00:20:03.550 --> 00:20:06.340
and as they become available.

00:20:06.340 --> 00:20:09.205
So now that we
talked about Cook,

00:20:09.205 --> 00:20:10.830
we can start talking
about autoscaling,

00:20:10.830 --> 00:20:13.032
because Cook and the
autoscaler work together.

00:20:13.032 --> 00:20:15.240
And it's kind of the next
logical step in this thing.

00:20:15.240 --> 00:20:17.710
So I think I
mentioned this before.

00:20:17.710 --> 00:20:21.240
The first version
we wrote in 2016.

00:20:21.240 --> 00:20:23.400
One of the reasons why
we wrote the autoscaler

00:20:23.400 --> 00:20:26.130
is because we wanted to
have a deep integration

00:20:26.130 --> 00:20:28.440
with the rest of the
Two Sigma ecosystem,

00:20:28.440 --> 00:20:30.780
in particular Cook and Mesos.

00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:33.150
And then we also wanted
to take the opportunity

00:20:33.150 --> 00:20:37.260
to give Cook the right resources
by creating the right machine

00:20:37.260 --> 00:20:38.880
types.

00:20:38.880 --> 00:20:42.340
Over time as we
[INAUDIBLE] on Launchy,

00:20:42.340 --> 00:20:44.950
we've started to use more,
and more, and more custom

00:20:44.950 --> 00:20:46.120
machine types.

00:20:46.120 --> 00:20:49.226
And now we're at a point where
we almost exclusively use

00:20:49.226 --> 00:20:50.100
custom machine types.

00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:53.700
And then the other thing is,
as the usage of the cloud grew,

00:20:53.700 --> 00:20:57.540
as we started to move more and
more workloads into the cloud,

00:20:57.540 --> 00:20:59.280
we had to expand to
multiple regions.

00:20:59.280 --> 00:21:00.930
And we had to expand
to multiple zones

00:21:00.930 --> 00:21:02.760
to try to avoid preemption.

00:21:02.760 --> 00:21:06.270
And we had to write some code
here and there into Launchy

00:21:06.270 --> 00:21:08.640
to be able to kind of
chase this capacity.

00:21:08.640 --> 00:21:11.774
Because you can have a pretty
large cluster in one zone.

00:21:11.774 --> 00:21:13.440
And then you could
lose some percentage.

00:21:13.440 --> 00:21:14.606
But you still have the jobs.

00:21:14.606 --> 00:21:16.500
So you need to run
those jobs somewhere.

00:21:16.500 --> 00:21:18.960
And Launchy and
Cook together, they

00:21:18.960 --> 00:21:23.280
allow us to do these
types of things.

00:21:23.280 --> 00:21:26.310
A high level overview of Launchy
is essentially a big loop.

00:21:26.310 --> 00:21:27.893
The first thing that
we're going to do

00:21:27.893 --> 00:21:29.690
is check for idle
or unhealthy VMs.

00:21:29.690 --> 00:21:31.640
This is essentially
scaling down.

00:21:31.640 --> 00:21:34.050
If the VMs are idle,
just scale down.

00:21:34.050 --> 00:21:36.530
Then we're going to look
at the job queue in Cook.

00:21:36.530 --> 00:21:38.620
If there's something,
we keep it in memory.

00:21:38.620 --> 00:21:41.970
Otherwise, we just stop scaling.

00:21:41.970 --> 00:21:43.830
Then we check for
what we call limits.

00:21:43.830 --> 00:21:45.720
Limits here could be GCP quotas.

00:21:45.720 --> 00:21:48.180
It could be internal
limits, like don't

00:21:48.180 --> 00:21:50.160
spend more than X amount
of money per hour.

00:21:50.160 --> 00:21:54.714
Don't create more than X number
of VMs on a given time window.

00:21:54.714 --> 00:21:57.130
Because, remember, they have
to talk to internal services.

00:21:57.130 --> 00:22:04.090
These services are kind of set
up for some maximum peak load.

00:22:04.090 --> 00:22:08.067
So we cannot just
start hammering them.

00:22:08.067 --> 00:22:10.650
If all the checks are good, if
all the checks and balances are

00:22:10.650 --> 00:22:14.460
good, then we compute what we
call a runnable subset of jobs.

00:22:14.460 --> 00:22:17.460
That essentially is,
look at the job queue.

00:22:17.460 --> 00:22:21.000
Let's say I'm a user, and I
have a quota of 1,000 jobs.

00:22:21.000 --> 00:22:22.800
And I already have
500 jobs running.

00:22:22.800 --> 00:22:25.260
If I put 2,000 more
jobs on the queue,

00:22:25.260 --> 00:22:27.390
obviously only 500 are
going to get scheduled.

00:22:27.390 --> 00:22:31.280
So we don't want to create
resources for 2,000 jobs.

00:22:31.280 --> 00:22:33.580
We only want to create
resources for 500 jobs.

00:22:33.580 --> 00:22:36.084
So that's what the
runnable subset means.

00:22:36.084 --> 00:22:37.500
Now that we have
the requirements,

00:22:37.500 --> 00:22:40.620
we can actually go and
compute the machine types

00:22:40.620 --> 00:22:42.420
that we want to use.

00:22:42.420 --> 00:22:44.490
The input of this
function is essentially

00:22:44.490 --> 00:22:46.380
just the list of requirements.

00:22:46.380 --> 00:22:50.010
And the output is almost a
fully well-formed payload

00:22:50.010 --> 00:22:51.390
that we can send to Google.

00:22:51.390 --> 00:22:55.860
So it's something like,
create 200 VMs of type X,

00:22:55.860 --> 00:23:00.070
and another 150 of type Y,
and something like that.

00:23:00.070 --> 00:23:02.666
And then the last thing,
to kind of wrap it up,

00:23:02.666 --> 00:23:04.290
we need to know where
to put these VMs.

00:23:04.290 --> 00:23:06.120
So the last step
in the scaling loop

00:23:06.120 --> 00:23:10.680
is figuring out where
to create these VMs.

00:23:10.680 --> 00:23:13.230
We have the payloads already.

00:23:13.230 --> 00:23:14.899
And we send a request to Google.

00:23:14.899 --> 00:23:16.690
Hopefully we get
everything that we wanted.

00:23:16.690 --> 00:23:18.720
And then we write
some more metrics

00:23:18.720 --> 00:23:21.000
to do further analysis offline.

00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:24.330
Or we're going to use
these metrics also

00:23:24.330 --> 00:23:27.630
in the distribution
calculation in the next loops.

00:23:31.374 --> 00:23:33.040
So if you put Cook
and Launchy together,

00:23:33.040 --> 00:23:34.930
this is pretty
much what you get.

00:23:34.930 --> 00:23:39.510
This is an intraday
graph of capacity.

00:23:39.510 --> 00:23:42.174
In Two Sigma teal you
have the used resources.

00:23:42.174 --> 00:23:43.840
And I don't know if
you can see it here,

00:23:43.840 --> 00:23:46.610
but there is a little
spike in green,

00:23:46.610 --> 00:23:48.250
which is provisioned resources.

00:23:48.250 --> 00:23:50.170
So there's usually
a little discrepancy

00:23:50.170 --> 00:23:53.600
between what we create and
what's actually being used,

00:23:53.600 --> 00:23:54.650
which is not a big deal.

00:23:54.650 --> 00:23:57.170
I think we're OK
living with that.

00:23:57.170 --> 00:24:01.060
But I think the more interesting
things here is kind of the fact

00:24:01.060 --> 00:24:05.020
that this graph
clearly is very spiky.

00:24:05.020 --> 00:24:07.240
And the spikes are
very short lived.

00:24:07.240 --> 00:24:10.750
So this data validates
our initial assumption

00:24:10.750 --> 00:24:14.680
that demand is very bursty,
and that we just should not

00:24:14.680 --> 00:24:19.720
have these kind of fixed
blocks of resources, business

00:24:19.720 --> 00:24:23.200
hours New York, or 24/7,
or something like that.

00:24:23.200 --> 00:24:25.720
Now, what's more annoying
about this is the fact

00:24:25.720 --> 00:24:31.810
that things like what's
your average cluster size

00:24:31.810 --> 00:24:33.550
doesn't really make sense here.

00:24:33.550 --> 00:24:37.120
I can give you a global max
over the last six months,

00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:40.252
and some kind of
local max interweek,

00:24:40.252 --> 00:24:41.210
or something like that.

00:24:41.210 --> 00:24:44.560
But average utilization
or average size

00:24:44.560 --> 00:24:46.090
doesn't really make much sense.

00:24:46.090 --> 00:24:49.360
And beyond the technicality
that I cannot give a straight

00:24:49.360 --> 00:24:54.530
number, this is important
because all these resources

00:24:54.530 --> 00:24:56.280
talk back to services
in Two Sigma.

00:24:56.280 --> 00:24:58.010
And people have to
do capacity planning.

00:24:58.010 --> 00:25:02.560
They need to know if their
service is going to have

00:25:02.560 --> 00:25:05.080
10 replicas or 20 replicas.

00:25:05.080 --> 00:25:06.536
And it creates these frictions.

00:25:06.536 --> 00:25:08.410
Because on the cloud
we're doing these things

00:25:08.410 --> 00:25:11.560
where we want to scale
up as much as possible.

00:25:11.560 --> 00:25:13.720
But internally, people
have limited resources,

00:25:13.720 --> 00:25:15.670
and they need to be
aware of these things.

00:25:18.210 --> 00:25:19.520
So let's talk about allocation.

00:25:19.520 --> 00:25:22.490
So if we recap, we've
talked about elasticity.

00:25:22.490 --> 00:25:25.220
We've talked about how
we can scale up and down,

00:25:25.220 --> 00:25:28.670
and give these
resources to our users.

00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:32.840
But we also need to do it
in a cost effective way.

00:25:32.840 --> 00:25:36.230
And allocation is just
one metric for this.

00:25:36.230 --> 00:25:38.840
Usually it hovers
between 90% to 95%.

00:25:38.840 --> 00:25:42.680
And we can achieve this
with Launchy and Cook.

00:25:42.680 --> 00:25:44.370
But there are these dips.

00:25:44.370 --> 00:25:45.962
Every once in a
while there are dips.

00:25:45.962 --> 00:25:48.170
And you might be thinking,
what's the deal with that?

00:25:48.170 --> 00:25:49.550
Like these guys
wrote an autoscaler,

00:25:49.550 --> 00:25:51.300
and they wrote a
scheduler, and they still

00:25:51.300 --> 00:25:54.380
have these pretty
bad inefficiencies.

00:25:54.380 --> 00:25:55.700
But they are explainable.

00:25:55.700 --> 00:26:00.170
And it's really
because the one thing

00:26:00.170 --> 00:26:03.800
that we try to optimize for
is to never have a backlog.

00:26:03.800 --> 00:26:05.187
We moved to the cloud.

00:26:05.187 --> 00:26:06.770
And we don't want
to be in a situation

00:26:06.770 --> 00:26:08.970
where someone has to
wait for resources.

00:26:08.970 --> 00:26:12.010
So granted, we have some
kind of restrictions

00:26:12.010 --> 00:26:14.000
in how fast we scale up.

00:26:14.000 --> 00:26:15.920
But we tried to
push that boundary

00:26:15.920 --> 00:26:21.930
as far as possible to kind of
provide a good user experience.

00:26:21.930 --> 00:26:23.900
So let me give you
an example of how you

00:26:23.900 --> 00:26:25.560
can end up in this situation.

00:26:25.560 --> 00:26:28.310
Suppose that T0,
nothing is happening.

00:26:28.310 --> 00:26:30.730
A couple of jobs
running on a single VM.

00:26:30.730 --> 00:26:33.610
We're at 100% allocation.

00:26:33.610 --> 00:26:35.480
Say at T1 a user
comes, and they want

00:26:35.480 --> 00:26:39.080
to run a couple of workloads
or a couple of jobs.

00:26:39.080 --> 00:26:40.076
Now we have a backlog.

00:26:40.076 --> 00:26:41.450
And this is exactly
the situation

00:26:41.450 --> 00:26:43.160
that we want to avoid.

00:26:43.160 --> 00:26:45.830
So Cook and Launchy
start doing their thing.

00:26:45.830 --> 00:26:46.820
They create some VMs.

00:26:46.820 --> 00:26:48.320
They bootstrap.

00:26:48.320 --> 00:26:49.460
They register to Mesos.

00:26:49.460 --> 00:26:50.990
And Cook schedules that.

00:26:50.990 --> 00:26:53.540
And now the user
is fully running.

00:26:53.540 --> 00:26:55.940
We drop to 90% allocation.

00:26:55.940 --> 00:26:58.400
For whatever reason, there
was a little inefficiency.

00:26:58.400 --> 00:26:59.490
Not such a big deal.

00:26:59.490 --> 00:27:01.490
But this is when it
gets interesting.

00:27:01.490 --> 00:27:04.520
Let's say half a day goes
by, and now we're at T50.

00:27:04.520 --> 00:27:07.140
Most of the job's finished.

00:27:07.140 --> 00:27:09.710
But there is still one
job in every single VM.

00:27:09.710 --> 00:27:11.870
And obviously, we cannot
terminate this VM,

00:27:11.870 --> 00:27:14.870
because we don't want to waste
this progress that we've made.

00:27:14.870 --> 00:27:16.020
So we keep them alive.

00:27:16.020 --> 00:27:17.510
And this is when
you see the dips.

00:27:17.510 --> 00:27:21.230
And this is when
allocation drops to 25%.

00:27:21.230 --> 00:27:25.230
But there is like a
twist to this example.

00:27:25.230 --> 00:27:27.950
Let's say really most
of the jobs finish.

00:27:27.950 --> 00:27:30.290
We terminate the VMs
that were fully idle.

00:27:30.290 --> 00:27:33.590
But there is still one
VM with one job running.

00:27:33.590 --> 00:27:35.820
And we're still
at 25% allocation.

00:27:35.820 --> 00:27:36.320
All right.

00:27:36.320 --> 00:27:38.180
So what actually happened here?

00:27:38.180 --> 00:27:40.580
What we found out over
time is that just looking

00:27:40.580 --> 00:27:43.400
at this percentage at face value
doesn't really make much sense.

00:27:43.400 --> 00:27:45.230
And it doesn't give
us the full picture.

00:27:45.230 --> 00:27:47.030
It's not the same
thing saying we

00:27:47.030 --> 00:27:50.870
wasted 75% of a 100,000 cores
cluster than saying we wasted

00:27:50.870 --> 00:27:54.560
75% of a 500 cores cluster.

00:27:54.560 --> 00:27:59.930
And we'd rather look at the
CPU hour and memory hour rates

00:27:59.930 --> 00:28:02.540
that we paid for but
we didn't actually use,

00:28:02.540 --> 00:28:05.630
rather than just
looking at this number.

00:28:05.630 --> 00:28:08.450
But more importantly,
I think is the fact

00:28:08.450 --> 00:28:13.310
that you have to keep in
mind that we want to avoid

00:28:13.310 --> 00:28:14.730
having a backlog at any time.

00:28:14.730 --> 00:28:17.610
We don't want people just
waiting for resources.

00:28:17.610 --> 00:28:22.370
So this allocation thing is
the second kind of priority.

00:28:22.370 --> 00:28:28.700
We optimize for it, but we
don't try to look good on paper

00:28:28.700 --> 00:28:31.280
over users having
a good experience.

00:28:31.280 --> 00:28:34.022
And this is just one
of the many challenges

00:28:34.022 --> 00:28:37.790
that we have on a
daily basis and that we

00:28:37.790 --> 00:28:40.340
have to deal with in
this elastic environment.

00:28:40.340 --> 00:28:43.220
But there's many, many more that
Chris is going to talk about.

00:28:48.480 --> 00:28:50.090
CHRIS FORTIER: Hello again.

00:28:50.090 --> 00:28:51.804
So we only have a
few minutes left.

00:28:51.804 --> 00:28:53.220
We know that we
are the last thing

00:28:53.220 --> 00:28:54.511
standing between you and lunch.

00:28:54.511 --> 00:28:57.187
So we'll try to get through
these fairly straightforward.

00:28:57.187 --> 00:28:59.270
So we talked about a lot
of the interesting things

00:28:59.270 --> 00:29:02.750
and the problems we've solved,
been our big challenges so far,

00:29:02.750 --> 00:29:04.400
and what we have left to solve.

00:29:04.400 --> 00:29:06.852
A lot of it does stem
from being hybrid cloud,

00:29:06.852 --> 00:29:08.810
and that we're using
dynamic compute in Google,

00:29:08.810 --> 00:29:11.050
but all of our data stores
are still on-prem somewhere.

00:29:11.050 --> 00:29:12.175
They're still a SQL server.

00:29:12.175 --> 00:29:13.730
They're still an
[INAUDIBLE] cluster.

00:29:13.730 --> 00:29:16.190
And getting all that
data from our data center

00:29:16.190 --> 00:29:18.876
into Google to do any
computation is a challenge.

00:29:18.876 --> 00:29:20.750
And we're still working
through the right way

00:29:20.750 --> 00:29:22.100
to solve that one.

00:29:22.100 --> 00:29:23.760
Especially with back
pressure as well.

00:29:23.760 --> 00:29:26.600
So the ability to spin
up a 5X data center

00:29:26.600 --> 00:29:28.330
is great for our users.

00:29:28.330 --> 00:29:30.149
They love that ability.

00:29:30.149 --> 00:29:31.940
Our network team, our
infrastructure teams,

00:29:31.940 --> 00:29:33.480
they don't love us
when we do that.

00:29:33.480 --> 00:29:36.500
They tend to get very upset when
we DDoS their services because

00:29:36.500 --> 00:29:37.830
of our users.

00:29:37.830 --> 00:29:41.380
So solving those types of
things, solving QoS and network

00:29:41.380 --> 00:29:44.670
fair share is something that
we haven't even looked at yet.

00:29:44.670 --> 00:29:47.836
So those are some of the things
to really solve and figure out.

00:29:47.836 --> 00:29:49.710
As Michael mentioned,
checkpoint and restore.

00:29:49.710 --> 00:29:53.150
So we talked about 15 years
of history of Two Sigma of how

00:29:53.150 --> 00:29:54.470
we've done research.

00:29:54.470 --> 00:29:56.010
Some of the stuff
that was built 10,

00:29:56.010 --> 00:29:58.144
12 years ago, we're
still running.

00:29:58.144 --> 00:30:00.185
And it doesn't necessarily
support checkpointing.

00:30:00.185 --> 00:30:01.970
It doesn't support restoring.

00:30:01.970 --> 00:30:03.851
So in those types of
use cases, there's

00:30:03.851 --> 00:30:05.600
stuff we can do from
the engineering side.

00:30:05.600 --> 00:30:06.975
But there's also
stuff we go talk

00:30:06.975 --> 00:30:09.359
to our users to figure out,
can their system handle it?

00:30:09.359 --> 00:30:10.900
Can they go rewrite
and add a feature

00:30:10.900 --> 00:30:13.010
to checkpoint to
somewhere, and then

00:30:13.010 --> 00:30:17.140
figure out how to make
their life more dynamic?

00:30:17.140 --> 00:30:18.470
So some of the takeaways.

00:30:18.470 --> 00:30:22.954
To get through those, we are
able to do a lot of things

00:30:22.954 --> 00:30:24.245
now that we couldn't do before.

00:30:24.245 --> 00:30:28.670
Like I said, the ability to spin
up data centers despite an API

00:30:28.670 --> 00:30:30.860
call, the optionality of it.

00:30:30.860 --> 00:30:34.320
What's the ability for me to
land a TPU in a data center?

00:30:34.320 --> 00:30:34.820
None.

00:30:34.820 --> 00:30:35.570
It's not possible.

00:30:35.570 --> 00:30:38.370
But with Google, I can
just make it an API call.

00:30:38.370 --> 00:30:39.620
Oh, add a TPU here.

00:30:39.620 --> 00:30:41.334
Add 50 GPUs over here.

00:30:41.334 --> 00:30:44.000
It's something that our modelers
are really, really looking for,

00:30:44.000 --> 00:30:46.340
and it opens up a ton of
research possibilities

00:30:46.340 --> 00:30:47.160
for them.

00:30:47.160 --> 00:30:49.910
Our investments in things
like Cook and our autoscaler

00:30:49.910 --> 00:30:52.790
have really paid off and
opened a bunch of value.

00:30:52.790 --> 00:30:54.680
But then the takeaway
is also, hybrid cloud

00:30:54.680 --> 00:30:56.920
introduces a lot of frictions,
which a lot of people

00:30:56.920 --> 00:30:59.660
have talked about in trying
to operate both environments.

00:30:59.660 --> 00:31:02.810
There's many unsolved problems.

00:31:02.810 --> 00:31:05.720
And then improving scheduling
is a never ending quest.

00:31:05.720 --> 00:31:08.450
A lot of the optionality, custom
instance types, for example,

00:31:08.450 --> 00:31:10.250
isn't something that
we had two years ago.

00:31:10.250 --> 00:31:12.300
So the idea to go
through and say, oh,

00:31:12.300 --> 00:31:15.230
I want X number of
CPU, X number of RAM

00:31:15.230 --> 00:31:16.580
is a really interesting feature.

00:31:16.580 --> 00:31:18.950
But every time these come
out is another optimization

00:31:18.950 --> 00:31:22.440
that we can make
in our environment.

00:31:22.440 --> 00:31:25.580
The obligatory-- we are hiring
across the platform engineering

00:31:25.580 --> 00:31:29.110
teams, our cloud security
teams, and even some of our data

00:31:29.110 --> 00:31:29.720
teams.

00:31:29.720 --> 00:31:32.390
The teams that
are actually using

00:31:32.390 --> 00:31:35.960
some of these services really
hardcore are also hiring.

00:31:35.960 --> 00:31:37.322
We have lots of openings.

00:31:37.322 --> 00:31:39.530
The slide earlier-- there
are about nine talks by Two

00:31:39.530 --> 00:31:40.554
Sigma this week.

00:31:40.554 --> 00:31:42.470
Feel free to check out
some of the other ones.

00:31:42.470 --> 00:31:44.516
Some of them are
more PE focused.

00:31:44.516 --> 00:31:46.140
Some of them are more
security focused.

00:31:46.140 --> 00:31:47.960
Some of them are
more data focused.

00:31:47.960 --> 00:31:49.935
So we recommend going
to see some of those.

00:31:49.935 --> 00:31:51.560
And then last but
not least, thank you.

00:31:51.560 --> 00:31:54.410
I think we have about
five minutes left for Q&amp;A.

00:31:54.410 --> 00:31:57.160
[MUSIC PLAYING]

